"<h2 id=\"study-guide\">0. How to study this topic</h2>\n<p>Statistics begins before calculation. First, we need to know which question we want to answer, what we have measured and how the data were organised. An appropriate table or graph reveals patterns; careful interpretation prevents us from attributing more meaning to the numbers than they actually contain.</p>\n<p>By the end, you should be able to identify a population and a sample, distinguish a parameter from a statistic, classify variables, read a matrix, construct frequencies, work with intervals and choose graphical representations. You should also be able to recognise differences in centre, spread and shape visually.</p>\n<div class=\"tema1-box\"><strong>Study route.</strong> Read the theory; follow the example; reproduce the procedure without looking; answer the understanding check. If you make a mistake, identify which decision went wrong: classification, denominator, accumulation, boundaries or interpretation. Return to that step and try again.</div>\n<p><strong>What comes from the textbook:</strong> the concepts, the conventions of Tema 1 and the anxiety distribution for 40 students. <strong>What is new:</strong> the mini-case involving 12 students, the exercises written here and the educational graphs identified as such. The data in the textbook's own Example 1.1 are also fictional and educational; they are not presented as actual clinical findings.</p>\n<p>We will not yet calculate the mean, standard deviation, skewness coefficients or hypothesis tests. Tema 1 prepares you to understand them. You do not need to memorise methods that have not yet been explained.</p>\n<p class=\"ref\">Textbook: introduction and objectives, printed pp. 3–4 / PDF pp. 1–2; fictional nature of the example, printed p. 20 / PDF p. 18.</p>\n<h2 id=\"statistics\">1. Statistics in psychological research</h2>\n<h3>1.1. Why analyse data?</h3>\n<p>In Psychology, we observe behaviour, record responses and measure characteristics. Statistics helps us organise that information, summarise it and assess which conclusions are justified. Theoretical statistics (estadística teórica) develops the foundations of the procedures; applied statistics (estadística aplicada) uses those procedures to address specific questions.</p>\n<p>The scientific method (método científico) is systematic: it follows explicit procedures. It is replicable: other researchers must be able to examine how the evidence was obtained and repeat the procedure under comparable conditions. A numerical result needs a question and a context before it can become a psychological conclusion.</p>\n<h3>1.2. A psychological example connecting research and analysis</h3>\n<p>Example 1.1 in the textbook asks whether a programme combining study techniques and relaxation improves the performance of students with examination anxiety. There are 40 volunteer students; 20 are randomly assigned to the group without treatment and 20 to the group receiving treatment. Scores and other relevant characteristics are recorded.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Stage</th><th scope=\"col\">Question it answers</th><th scope=\"col\">Application to the example</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>1. Statement of the problem</td><td>What do we need to find out?</td><td>Whether the programme improves examination performance.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2. Formulation of hypotheses</td><td>What outcome do we expect and can we test?</td><td>That the group receiving the programme will perform better.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3. Design and data collection</td><td>Whom do we observe and how do we measure?</td><td>Define measures, select participants and assign conditions.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4. Data analysis</td><td>How do we summarise and examine the results?</td><td>Organise scores and describe distributions; more advanced comparisons are studied later.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5. Discussion</td><td>What do the results mean and what are their limitations?</td><td>Relate the results to the hypothesis and consider explanations and limitations.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6. Report</td><td>How do we communicate the procedure and evidence?</td><td>Explain methods, results and limitations so that they can be reviewed.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><div class=\"tema1-box\"><strong>Do not confuse these:</strong> volunteering describes how a person enters the study; random assignment to a group describes which condition they receive. Random assignment does not, by itself, make a volunteer sample representative of the whole population.</div>\n<h3>1.3. Description and inference are different tasks</h3>\n<p><strong>Descriptive statistics (estadística descriptiva):</strong> organises and summarises observed data using tables, graphs and indices. “Among these 40 students, 25 have anxiety scores of 10 or less” describes the sample.</p>\n<p><strong>Inferential statistics (estadística inferencial):</strong> uses probabilistic procedures to draw conclusions about a population from a sample. It requires consideration of assumptions, uncertainty, the selection procedure and measurement quality. Simply replacing “these students” with “all students” is not sufficient.</p>\n<p>The combined anxiety distribution of the 40 participants does not, by itself, demonstrate that the programme is effective: to answer that question, we need information about the groups and the design. Tema 1 teaches us to organise and describe, rather than to perform that test yet.</p>\n<details class=\"check\"><summary>Check: does 25 out of 40 demonstrate that the programme works?</summary><p>No. It summarises a variable in the observed group. The relevant comparison between conditions and its appropriate analysis are missing.</p></details>\n<p class=\"ref\">Textbook: printed p. 3 / PDF p. 1; method and Example 1.1, printed pp. 5–7 / PDF pp. 3–5; description and inference, printed pp. 7–10 / PDF pp. 5–8. The warning about selection and assignment is a methodological clarification by Sensus Vitae.</p>\n<h2 id=\"population\">2. Population, sample, parameter and statistic</h2>\n<p>The <strong>population (población)</strong> is the set of units to which the question refers. It must be defined: “students enrolled in a course during a particular year” is more precise than “students”. The <strong>sample (muestra)</strong> is the subset actually observed. It can be large and still biased if its selection systematically excludes certain people.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">What it describes</th><th scope=\"col\">Example: study time</th><th scope=\"col\">Textbook notation</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Population (población)</td><td>All units of interest</td><td>Everyone enrolled in the defined course and year</td><td>It is not a numerical index</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sample (muestra)</td><td>The observed units</td><td>Students whose time was recorded</td><td>n: sample size</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Parameter (parámetro)</td><td>A numerical characteristic of the population</td><td>Mean time of everyone enrolled</td><td>μ; σ²<sub>X</sub>; π</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Statistic (estadístico)</td><td>A characteristic calculated in the sample</td><td>Mean time of those observed</td><td>X̄; S²<sub>X</sub>; P</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p>In this table, μ and X̄ represent means; σ²<sub>X</sub> and S²<sub>X</sub>, variances; π and P, proportions. Here, it is enough to recognise which set they refer to: the formulae for these indices are studied later. Notation varies between books.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example.</strong> We want to know the proportion of all students in the course who work. We ask 100, and 30 answer “yes”. The sample consists of the 100 observed students; 30/100 = 0,30 is a sample proportion and therefore a statistic. The proportion in the whole population is a parameter that we have not observed directly. Knowing the statistic does not guarantee that it equals the parameter.</p>\n<div class=\"tema1-box\"><strong>Watch the symbol P:</strong> the textbook uses P to denote a sample proportion when introducing parameters and statistics. In frequency tables, it uses Pᵢ for percentages. Context and subscripts matter; 0,30 and 30% express the same proportion in different formats.</div>\n<details class=\"check\"><summary>Check: is 62,5% calculated from the 40 participants a parameter or a statistic?</summary><p>A statistic. It is calculated in the sample. It would only be a parameter if it described the entire population we have defined.</p></details>\n<p class=\"ref\">Textbook: printed pp. 7–10 / PDF pp. 5–8; symbols and definitions, printed p. 10 / PDF p. 8. The example involving 100 students is new and educational.</p>\n<h2 id=\"measurement\">3. Variables: measurement and classification</h2>\n<h3>3.1. What a variable is and how it is represented</h3>\n<p>A <strong>variable (variable)</strong> comprises the possible values of a characteristic when it is measured in the units of a population or sample. For example, “reaction time” is the variable; “487 milliseconds” is the value recorded for one person.</p>\n<p>We represent a variable with a capital letter, such as X. The symbol Xᵢ denotes the value of X in case i. In a sample of n cases, we write X₁, X₂, …, Xₙ. If X is anxiety and the first student scores 2 points, X₁ = 2. The letter identifies what we measure; the subscript identifies the person in whom we record it.</p>\n<p><strong>Measurement (medición)</strong> consists of assigning numbers to objects or characteristics according to rules. The rules determine what those numbers mean. A code “2” may identify a group; a time of “2 seconds” expresses a quantity. Writing both as numbers does not justify performing the same operations on them.</p>\n<p>For psychological characteristics, we do not observe “anxiety” directly in the way we observe a length. We record responses, behaviours or scores using a defined procedure. We need to state which indicator we use and how it is obtained.</p>\n<p class=\"ref\">Textbook: printed pp. 10–12 / PDF pp. 8–10.</p>\n<h3>3.2. The four scales of measurement</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Scale</th><th scope=\"col\">What comparisons it permits</th><th scope=\"col\">Psychological example</th><th scope=\"col\">What it does not permit us to conclude</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Nominal (nominal)</strong></td><td>Equality or difference between categories</td><td>Group: 1 = without programme, 2 = with programme</td><td>2 does not mean “twice as much” as 1. The codes are labels.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Ordinal (ordinal)</strong></td><td>Equality/difference and order</td><td>Low, medium or high severity</td><td>The distance between levels need not be equal.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Interval (de intervalo)</strong></td><td>Order and equal differences on the scale</td><td>Psychological test scores according to the convention of this Tema 1; temperature in °C as a physical example</td><td>Zero is conventional. 40 points does not mean “twice the trait” of 20.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Ratio (de razón)</strong></td><td>Equal differences and ratios, with an absolute zero</td><td>Reaction time, number of errors, hours of study</td><td>“Twice as much” refers to the measured quantity, not automatically to a different psychological quality.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p>Example: 6 hours is twice 3 hours of study. However, that does not demonstrate that twice as much has been learned. A reaction time of 600 ms is twice one of 300 ms; it does not mean “half the intelligence” either.</p>\n<div class=\"tema1-box\"><strong>Textbook convention and methodological precision.</strong> In the tasks in this chapter, psychological test results are treated as interval measurements when the question presents them that way. The textbook uses this convention when classifying intelligence, anxiety and psychological flexibility. In actual research, writing a response numerically does not, by itself, demonstrate equal intervals: this depends on the instrument and its justification. An ordered agreement item is ordinal; it should not automatically be confused with a total test score. A zero on a test does not demonstrate complete absence of the trait either.</div>\n<p class=\"ref\">Textbook: scales, printed pp. 12–17 / PDF pp. 10–15; Table 1.1, printed p. 18 / PDF p. 16; ordinal item and test score, solutions printed pp. 43–44 / PDF pp. 41–42. The point about justifying intervals is a methodological clarification.</p>\n<h3>3.3. Variable type and scale: related classifications</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Terminology in Tema 1</th><th scope=\"col\">Related scale</th><th scope=\"col\">Example</th><th scope=\"col\">Question for identifying it</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Qualitative or categorical (cualitativa o categórica)</td><td>Nominal</td><td>Type of treatment</td><td>Does it identify categories without a quantitative order?</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Quasi-quantitative (cuasicuantitativa)</td><td>Ordinal</td><td>Degree of satisfaction</td><td>Can I order the values without measuring equal distances?</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Quantitative (cuantitativa)</td><td>Interval or ratio</td><td>Time; a test score according to the stated convention</td><td>Do the values permit comparisons of quantities or differences?</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p>“Quasi-quantitative” (cuasicuantitativa) is the textbook's name for ordinal variables. In other texts, you may find “ordinal categorical variable”. To recognise UNED questions, retain the relationship in Table 1.1; do not switch terminology without noticing.</p>\n<p><strong>Dichotomous (dicotómica):</strong> has exactly two possible categories or values, such as “receives/does not receive the programme”. <strong>Polytomous (politómica):</strong> has more than two, such as three types of intervention. This distinction counts possibilities; it does not, by itself, establish distances between them.</p>\n<p><strong>Dichotomising (dicotomizar)</strong> means transforming a variable into two categories according to a rule. <strong>Polytomising (politomizar)</strong> means transforming it into more than two. For example, recoding a score as low/medium/high produces ordered categories. The cut-off points must be defined, and detail is lost: two different scores may end up in the same category.</p>\n<h3>3.4. Discrete and continuous: consider the variable, not just how it is written</h3>\n<p>A <strong>discrete (discreta)</strong> quantitative variable has separated values: 0, 1 or 2 errors, but not 1,5 errors in that count. A <strong>continuous (continua)</strong> variable can take intermediate values between two values within its range, such as a duration.</p>\n<p>If the device records time in whole milliseconds, the duration remains continuous; the recording has finite resolution. Counting errors and rounding a time are different processes. Nor is every variable written without decimal places discrete.</p>\n<h3>3.5. Operational definition and role in the study</h3>\n<p>The <strong>operational definition (definición operativa)</strong> specifies how a characteristic is defined and recorded. “Knowledge” may be measured as a score interpreted on an interval scale; “number of correct answers” is a count with an absolute zero and a ratio scale. Zero correct answers means no answer was correct on that test, not complete absence of knowledge.</p>\n<p>In an experiment, the <strong>independent variable (variable independiente)</strong> is the condition manipulated or assigned; the <strong>dependent variable (variable dependiente)</strong> is the response measured. In the programme example, the treatment condition is independent and performance is a response of interest. In an observational study, a predictor may simply be observed: calling it a predictor does not demonstrate causality. Other factors may confound the relationship.</p>\n<p>Role and scale are different questions: a treatment group can be independent and nominal; a time can be dependent, ratio-scaled and continuous.</p>\n<details class=\"check\"><summary>Check: does “satisfaction, from 1 = low to 3 = high” become quantitative because it uses numbers?</summary><p>No. The codes express order. Without justification for equal distances, it is ordinal and quasi-quantitative in the terminology of Tema 1.</p></details>\n<p class=\"ref\">Textbook: dichotomy, polytomy and recoding, printed pp. 13–14 / PDF pp. 11–12; operational definition and discrete/continuous variables, printed pp. 17–18 / PDF pp. 15–16. The independent/dependent explanation retains a useful methodological extension from the published version; it is not attributed to a formal definition on those pages.</p>\n<h2 id=\"data-matrix\">4. From observations to the data matrix</h2>\n<h3>4.1. A mini-case for learning the procedure</h3>\n<p>We will use a new set of <strong>12 fictional cases</strong> to learn how to organise data from a study of examination anxiety. It is neither a selection nor a reconstruction of the textbook's 40 participants. The results we obtain describe only this mini-case.</p>\n<p>Each row represents a student; each column represents a variable. The identifier ID is a nominal label: student 12 does not have “twice as much” of a characteristic as student 6. The group codes are 1 = without programme and 2 = with programme. “Anxiety” represents a test score, following the interval convention of Tema 1; “errors” is a count and “hours” is a duration.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table><caption>New educational matrix: n = 12, with no missing data</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">ID</th><th scope=\"col\">Group</th><th scope=\"col\">Anxiety X</th><th scope=\"col\">Errors</th><th scope=\"col\">Hours of study</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1</td><td>4</td><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2</td><td>5</td><td>2</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1</td><td>7</td><td>0</td><td>2,5</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>2</td><td>8</td><td>3</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>1</td><td>9</td><td>1</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>2</td><td>10</td><td>2</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>1</td><td>12</td><td>2</td><td>4,5</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>2</td><td>14</td><td>4</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>1</td><td>18</td><td>3</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>2</td><td>24</td><td>1</td><td>3,5</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p>To read a cell, identify the row and column. In case 5, X₅ = 7, there were 0 errors and 2,5 hours were recorded. Do not confuse the 5 in the ID with the anxiety value.</p>\n<h3>4.2. Code and clean the data before calculating</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Check</th><th scope=\"col\">Example</th><th scope=\"col\">Reasoned decision</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Permitted codes</td><td>Group = 7, when only 1 and 2 exist</td><td>Check against the original record; do not invent a category.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Missing value (dato perdido)</td><td>Hours were not recorded</td><td>Mark it as missing; do not replace it with zero without justification.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Impossible value</td><td>−3 errors</td><td>Check for a possible recording mistake.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Outlier (dato atípico)</td><td>A time very different from the others</td><td>Investigate whether it is an error or a valid observation; document how it is handled.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Denominator</td><td>12 cases, but only 11 valid records of hours</td><td>State whether frequencies use 11 valid values or include a category for missing values.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><div class=\"tema1-box\"><strong>Methodological clarification.</strong> An extreme value is not automatically incorrect. The textbook mentions the usual exclusion of outliers; here, we avoid turning this into an automatic rule: review, justification and documentation are necessary. Tema 1 does not teach imputation or an advanced detection procedure.</div>\n<details class=\"check\"><summary>Check: is a blank space under “errors” equivalent to 0?</summary><p>No. Zero indicates that zero errors were observed; a blank indicates that the value is missing. Mixing them changes the distribution.</p></details>\n<p class=\"ref\">Textbook: matrix, coding, missing values and outliers, printed pp. 19–21 / PDF pp. 17–19. The 12-case matrix and checking examples are new.</p>\n<h2 id=\"frequencies\">5. Frequency distributions, step by step</h2>\n<h3>5.1. From many records to a summary</h3>\n<p>A <strong>frequency distribution (distribución de frecuencias)</strong> indicates how many observations correspond to each value, category or class. It must avoid overlaps and make clear which cases are included. We use n for the total number of observations analysed, k for the number of values or classes, and nᵢ for the number of observations in class i.</p>\n<p>In this section, i identifies a category or class in the table. In Xᵢ in the variables section, it identified an individual case. These are different uses of the subscript; read what has been defined in each context.</p>\n<div class=\"tema1-equations\" aria-label=\"Frequency formulas\">\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\" aria-label=\"∑i=1k nᵢ = n   ·   pᵢ = nᵢ / n   ·   Pᵢ = 100 pᵢ\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>·</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>·</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\" aria-label=\"na,i = ∑j=1i nⱼ   ·   pa,i = na,i / n = ∑j=1i pⱼ   ·   Pa,i = 100 pa,i\"><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>·</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>·</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math></div>\n<p><strong>Absolute frequency (frecuencia absoluta)</strong> is a count of cases; <strong>relative frequency (frecuencia relativa)</strong> is a proportion; the <strong>percentage (porcentaje)</strong> is that proportion multiplied by 100. The sign ∑ means summation: the first formula asks you to add all the frequencies in the table. The index a denotes accumulation. We add i to clarify up to which class; the textbook also uses nₐ, pₐ, Pₐ.</p>\n<h3>5.2. Complete example: number of errors</h3>\n<p>In the mini-case, we observe: <strong>0, 1, 1, 2, 0, 3, 1, 2, 2, 4, 3, 1</strong>.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Identify the variable.</strong> It is a count: quantitative, discrete and ratio-scaled.</li><li><strong>Count.</strong> 0 appears 2 times; 1, 4 times; 2, 3 times; 3, 2 times; and 4, 1 time.</li><li><strong>Calculate the proportion.</strong> For one error: pᵢ = 4/12 = 0,333…</li><li><strong>Convert to a percentage.</strong> 100 × 4/12 = 33,333…%.</li><li><strong>Accumulate in order.</strong> Up to one error: 2 + 4 = 6; up to two: 6 + 3 = 9.</li><li><strong>Check and interpret.</strong> The sum is 12. “At most two errors” includes 0, 1 and 2: 9/12 = 75%.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Errors</th><th scope=\"col\">nᵢ</th><th scope=\"col\">pᵢ</th><th scope=\"col\">Pᵢ (%)</th><th scope=\"col\">nₐ</th><th scope=\"col\">Pₐ (%)</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>0</td><td>2</td><td>0,1667</td><td>16,67</td><td>2</td><td>16,67</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>4</td><td>0,3333</td><td>33,33</td><td>6</td><td>50,00</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>0,2500</td><td>25,00</td><td>9</td><td>75,00</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>2</td><td>0,1667</td><td>16,67</td><td>11</td><td>91,67</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1</td><td>0,0833</td><td>8,33</td><td>12</td><td>100,00</td></tr><tr class=\"total\"><td>Total</td><td>12</td><td>1</td><td>100</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p>The decimal figures are rounded. Always calculate from the original counts: adding already rounded percentages can produce small differences. “Exactly two errors” is 3/12 = 25%; “two or fewer” is 9/12 = 75%. They answer different questions.</p>\n<h3>5.3. Nominal and ordinal: what changes?</h3>\n<p>The assigned group in the mini-case is nominal: there are 6 cases in each group, pᵢ = 0,50 and Pᵢ = 50%. Accumulating “up to group 2” as though treatments were ordered quantities makes no sense.</p>\n<p>We now recode anxiety for an educational demonstration: <strong>low = 1–5; medium = 6–15; high = more than 15</strong>. These cut-off points are invented for teaching; they are neither clinical thresholds nor textbook instructions. We obtain 4, 6 and 2 cases.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">nᵢ</th><th scope=\"col\">Pᵢ (%)</th><th scope=\"col\">nₐ</th><th scope=\"col\">Pₐ (%)</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Low</td><td>4</td><td>33,33</td><td>4</td><td>33,33</td></tr><tr><td>Medium</td><td>6</td><td>50,00</td><td>10</td><td>83,33</td></tr><tr><td>High</td><td>2</td><td>16,67</td><td>12</td><td>100,00</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p>The new variable is ordinal: we can accumulate from low to high. “Medium or below” includes 10/12 = 83,333…%. Retaining the order does not mean that the distance from low to medium equals that from medium to high.</p>\n<figure class=\"tema1-figure\"><a href=\"/study/data-analysis/tema1/en/fig01.svg\" aria-label=\"Open the full-size graph\" class=\"tema1-graph-link\"><img src=\"/study/data-analysis/tema1/en/fig01.svg\" alt=\"Figure SV1. Nominal and ordinal bars from the same mini-case, n=12. Heights: 6/6 and 4/6/2. Gaps separate categories; the second graph retains their order.\" aria-describedby=\"tema1-caption-1\" width=\"864\" height=\"324\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption id=\"tema1-caption-1\"><strong>Figure SV1.</strong> Nominal and ordinal bars from the same mini-case, n=12. Heights: 6/6 and 4/6/2. Gaps separate categories; the second graph retains their order.</figcaption><p class=\"ref\"><a href=\"/study/data-analysis/tema1/en/fig01.svg\">Open the full-size graph</a></p></figure>\n<h3>5.4. Recovering frequencies and checking</h3>\n<p>If you know cumulative frequencies, you recover an ordinary frequency by subtracting the preceding cumulative frequency: <strong>nᵢ = n<sub>a,i</sub> − n<sub>a,i−1</sub></strong>, taking the initial cumulative frequency as 0. For example, for two errors in the errors table: 9 − 6 = 3, not 9.</p>\n<div class=\"tema1-box\"><strong>Checklist:</strong> the nᵢ sum to n; the pᵢ sum to 1 before rounding; the Pᵢ sum to 100%; cumulative frequencies do not decrease; the final cumulative frequency is n, 1 or 100%, depending on its format. You do not add all the cumulative frequencies together to obtain the total.</div>\n<details class=\"check\"><summary>Check: in the ordinal table, does 50% mean “medium level or below”?</summary><p>No. 50% has exactly the medium level. “Medium or below” is 83,333…% because it includes low and medium.</p></details>\n<p class=\"ref\">Textbook: qualitative/ordinal distributions and graphs, printed pp. 22–27 / PDF pp. 20–25; definitions and accumulation, printed p. 26 / PDF p. 24; recovery by differences, printed p. 44 / PDF p. 42. Examples and Figure SV1: new educational material.</p>\n<h2 id=\"grouping\">6. Grouping, class limits and interval width</h2>\n<h3>6.1. Why we group and what we lose</h3>\n<p>When there are many values, a class brings several observations together and makes the overall pattern easier to see. However, grouping means losing detail: knowing how many people fall between 6 and 10 does not tell us how many scored exactly 7.</p>\n<p>The textbook permits different choices of class number, starting point and width. It does not prescribe k ≈ √n or R = (X<sub>máx</sub> − X<sub>mín</sub>) + u as compulsory rules. In the anxiety example, values range from 2 to 30; the observed range is 30 − 2 = 28, but the chosen intervals start at 1 and extend to 30.</p>\n<ol><li>Identify the recording unit and the range of values.</li><li>Choose clear classes that cover every case without overlaps.</li><li>Define their boundaries and the rule for boundary values.</li><li>Count each observation only once.</li><li>Calculate frequencies and check totals.</li><li>Explain that the chosen grouping is not the only possible one.</li></ol>\n<p class=\"ref\">Textbook: printed pp. 28–29 / PDF pp. 26–27. The working sequence is an educational organisation of these decisions.</p>\n<h3>6.2. Textbook example: anxiety in 40 students</h3>\n<p>The following table retains the correct data from Tables 1.5 and 1.6. We add ordinary percentages to distinguish them from cumulative percentages. This table uses <strong>n=40</strong>, not the 12 cases in the mini-case.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table class=\"wide\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Class</th><th scope=\"col\">LIE–LSE</th><th scope=\"col\">PM</th><th scope=\"col\">nᵢ</th><th scope=\"col\">pᵢ</th><th scope=\"col\">Pᵢ (%)</th><th scope=\"col\">nₐ</th><th scope=\"col\">pₐ</th><th scope=\"col\">Pₐ (%)</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>1–5</td><td>0,5–5,5</td><td>3</td><td>13</td><td>0,325</td><td>32,5</td><td>13</td><td>0,325</td><td>32,5</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6–10</td><td>5,5–10,5</td><td>8</td><td>12</td><td>0,300</td><td>30,0</td><td>25</td><td>0,625</td><td>62,5</td></tr>\n<tr><td>11–15</td><td>10,5–15,5</td><td>13</td><td>8</td><td>0,200</td><td>20,0</td><td>33</td><td>0,825</td><td>82,5</td></tr>\n<tr><td>16–20</td><td>15,5–20,5</td><td>18</td><td>4</td><td>0,100</td><td>10,0</td><td>37</td><td>0,925</td><td>92,5</td></tr>\n<tr><td>21–25</td><td>20,5–25,5</td><td>23</td><td>2</td><td>0,050</td><td>5,0</td><td>39</td><td>0,975</td><td>97,5</td></tr>\n<tr><td>26–30</td><td>25,5–30,5</td><td>28</td><td>1</td><td>0,025</td><td>2,5</td><td>40</td><td>1,000</td><td>100,0</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"total\"><td>Total</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>40</td><td>1</td><td>100</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p><strong>Worked reading:</strong> 12 people are in 6–10: 12/40 = 30%. Those scoring 10 or less are in the first two classes: (13+12)/40 = 62,5%. Those scoring above 10 number 40−25 = 15: 37,5%.</p>\n<p>For a value on a continuous boundary, we use one consistent rule, for example [0,5; 5,5) and [5,5; 10,5): include the left endpoint and exclude the right. Semicolons separate endpoints because we use a decimal comma. Integer records do not fall on these half-point boundaries. The inclusion convention is an educational clarification, not a new compulsory UNED rule.</p>\n<p class=\"ref\">Textbook: Tables 1.5–1.6, printed pp. 29–30 / PDF pp. 27–28.</p>\n<h3>6.3. Apparent limits, exact boundaries and midpoint</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">UNED textbook</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th><th scope=\"col\">Equivalent symbols</th><th scope=\"col\">Class 10–12, integer records</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>LIA</td><td>Lower apparent limit (límite inferior aparente)</td><td>L<sub>A</sub></td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>LSA</td><td>Upper apparent limit (límite superior aparente)</td><td>U<sub>A</sub></td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>LIE</td><td>Lower exact boundary (límite inferior exacto)</td><td>L<sub>E</sub></td><td>9,5</td></tr><tr><td>LSE</td><td>Upper exact boundary (límite superior exacto)</td><td>U<sub>E</sub></td><td>12,5</td></tr><tr><td>PM</td><td>Midpoint (punto medio)</td><td>M</td><td>11</td></tr><tr><td>Amplitud</td><td>Class width (anchura de clase)</td><td>w</td><td>3</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p>If the recording unit is u and values are rounded to that unit, we adjust each endpoint by half a unit:</p>\n<div class=\"tema1-equations\" aria-label=\"Class boundary formulas\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\" aria-label=\"LIE = LIA − u/2   ·   LSE = LSA + u/2\"><mrow><mi>LIE</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>LIA</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>·</mo><mi>LSE</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>LSA</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\" aria-label=\"PM = (LIE + LSE)/2 = (LIA + LSA)/2   ·   w = LSE − LIE\"><mrow><mi>PM</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>LIE</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>LSE</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>LIA</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>LSA</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>·</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>LSE</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>LIE</mi></mrow></math></div>\n<p>The equivalent symbols let you recognise the formulae and connect them to the textbook. Width is calculated between exact boundaries, not by subtracting only the apparent limits.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Recording</th><th scope=\"col\">u</th><th scope=\"col\">u/2</th><th scope=\"col\">Example</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Integers</td><td>1</td><td>0,5</td><td>10–12 → 9,5–12,5</td></tr><tr><td>One decimal place</td><td>0,1</td><td>0,05</td><td>3,0 → [2,95; 3,05)</td></tr><tr><td>Two decimal places</td><td>0,01</td><td>0,005</td><td>18,56 → [18,555; 18,565)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p><strong>Step by step, class 10–12:</strong> u=1; subtract 0,5 from 10 and add 0,5 to 12; obtain 9,5 and 12,5; w=12,5−9,5=3; PM=(9,5+12,5)/2=11. The apparent difference 12−10=2 is not its exact width.</p>\n<p><strong>Step by step, record 18,56:</strong> two decimal places → u=0,01; half a unit → 0,005; endpoints → 18,56−0,005=18,555 and 18,56+0,005=18,565. We represent [18,555; 18,565) using the stated convention. This describes the rounding of the record; it does not guarantee the instrument's physical accuracy.</p>\n<p class=\"ref\">Textbook: printed pp. 29–32 / PDF pp. 27–30; class 10–12, printed p. 44 / PDF p. 42; 18,56, printed p. 45 / PDF p. 43; PM, printed p. 46 / PDF p. 44. Example of 3,0: educational material derived from the rule.</p>\n<h3>6.4. Open-ended classes: one boundary is missing</h3>\n<p>An open-ended interval (intervalo abierto), in the terminology of grouping, may be an extreme class such as “400 or less” or “more than 500”: one of its boundaries is not specified. This is not the same as the mathematical open interval (a,b), which has two known endpoints but excludes them.</p>\n<p>We can count cases and calculate proportions in these classes. Without two boundaries, we do not know a finite width and cannot calculate the midpoint using the usual formula. We should not invent a boundary to draw a histogram as though it had been supplied.</p>\n<details class=\"check\"><summary>Check: is it correct to take 500 as the midpoint of “more than 500”?</summary><p>No. 500 is a threshold, not the centre of a class with two known limits.</p></details>\n<p class=\"ref\">Textbook: definition, printed p. 32 / PDF p. 30; open-ended classes in exercises and solutions, printed pp. 42 and 45–46 / PDF pp. 40 and 43–44.</p>\n<h2 id=\"graphs\">7. Graphical representations: constructing and reading</h2>\n<h3>7.1. The graph depends on what we have measured</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Data</th><th scope=\"col\">Representation</th><th scope=\"col\">What you must respect</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Nominal categories</td><td>Bars; pie sectors if they make proportions easier to compare</td><td>Clear labels; do not suggest a quantitative order.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ordinal categories</td><td>Ordered bars</td><td>Retain low/medium/high or the defined order.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ungrouped discrete values</td><td>Bars; line diagram or polygon with points</td><td>Points correspond to possible values; connecting them does not create intermediate values.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Grouped continuous measurements</td><td>Histogram; frequency polygon</td><td>Contiguous classes and clear boundaries; area represents frequency.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cumulative frequencies of ordered data</td><td>Bars or cumulative representations; cumulative curve according to the specified construction</td><td>Do not confuse a class frequency with the frequency up to that class.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p>For a pie chart (diagrama de sectores), the angle of each sector is <strong>360° × pᵢ</strong>. In the mini-case, each group contains 6/12=0,5: its sector would occupy 180°. Sectors for nominal categories are not accumulated as though those categories had an order.</p>\n<p>For nominal data, the most frequent category is identified as the mode (moda); for ordinal data, it makes sense to discuss ordered positions and the median (mediana). The textbook introduces this choice of indices before their calculations. Here, we do not calculate or develop indices belonging to subsequent topics.</p>\n<p class=\"ref\">Textbook: printed pp. 23–27 / PDF pp. 21–25; bars, lines, histograms and polygons, printed pp. 32–34 / PDF pp. 30–32. Selection table: educational synthesis.</p>\n<h3>7.2. A discrete count can have a polygon</h3>\n<p>The two representations below show exactly the same error counts from the mini-case. The textbook calls points joined at heights proportional to frequencies a <strong>line diagram (diagrama de líneas)</strong> or <strong>frequency polygon (polígono de frecuencias)</strong>. For discrete values, the line is a reading aid; it does not mean that 1,5 errors were observed.</p>\n<figure class=\"tema1-figure\"><a href=\"/study/data-analysis/tema1/en/fig02.svg\" aria-label=\"Open the full-size graph\" class=\"tema1-graph-link\"><img src=\"/study/data-analysis/tema1/en/fig02.svg\" alt=\"Figure SV2. New data, n=12: 0, 1, 2, 3 and 4 errors have frequencies 2, 4, 3, 2 and 1. Bars and polygon retain the same points.\" aria-describedby=\"tema1-caption-2\" width=\"864\" height=\"317\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption id=\"tema1-caption-2\"><strong>Figure SV2.</strong> New data, n=12: 0, 1, 2, 3 and 4 errors have frequencies 2, 4, 3, 2 and 1. Bars and polygon retain the same points.</figcaption><p class=\"ref\"><a href=\"/study/data-analysis/tema1/en/fig02.svg\">Open the full-size graph</a></p></figure>\n<p><strong>Construction:</strong> put values on X; set a frequency scale on Y starting at 0; draw each height; identify the variable and units. Before interpreting, read the Y-axis: it may contain counts, proportions, percentages or cumulative frequencies.</p>\n<h3>7.3. Histogram and polygon: the textbook's 40 cases</h3>\n<p>In a histogram (histograma), the rectangles occupy the intervals and touch because the boundaries are contiguous. Here, the classes have equal width 5; we can use nᵢ as the height because the areas 5nᵢ are proportional to the frequencies. The polygon on the right places each point above PM and uses pᵢ on the vertical axis.</p>\n<figure class=\"tema1-figure\"><a href=\"/study/data-analysis/tema1/en/fig03.svg\" aria-label=\"Open the full-size graph\" class=\"tema1-graph-link\"><img src=\"/study/data-analysis/tema1/en/fig03.svg\" alt=\"Figure SV3. New graphs constructed from Tables 1.5–1.6, printed pp. 29–30 / PDF pp. 27–28. Histogram: heights 13,12,8,4,2,1. Polygon: midpoints 3,8,13,18,23,28 and corresponding proportions. Conceptual basis: printed pp. 33–34 / PDF pp. 31–32.\" aria-describedby=\"tema1-caption-3\" width=\"864\" height=\"324\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption id=\"tema1-caption-3\"><strong>Figure SV3.</strong> New graphs constructed from Tables 1.5–1.6, printed pp. 29–30 / PDF pp. 27–28. Histogram: heights 13,12,8,4,2,1. Polygon: midpoints 3,8,13,18,23,28 and corresponding proportions. Conceptual basis: printed pp. 33–34 / PDF pp. 31–32.</figcaption><p class=\"ref\"><a href=\"/study/data-analysis/tema1/en/fig03.svg\">Open the full-size graph</a></p></figure>\n<ol><li>Obtain the exact boundaries: 0,5; 5,5; 10,5; …; 30,5.</li><li>For the histogram, use width 5 and the table frequencies as heights.</li><li>For the polygon, put the PM values on X and the pᵢ values on Y.</li><li>Check that you have not put 62,5% in the second class: its ordinary proportion is 30%; 62,5% is cumulative.</li><li>Interpret: the first intervals contain more cases; the figure does not give you each individual value.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"tema1-box\"><strong>A useful distinction:</strong> the same counts can be expressed in a table, a histogram and a polygon. Changing from counts to proportions changes the vertical scale but retains the relative information about the classes. No representation, by itself, recovers the detail lost through grouping.</div>\n<h3>7.4. Exact accumulation and accumulation of classes</h3>\n<p>In the mini-case, we know the individual scores: we can draw a step function that jumps at each observed value. At 10, it reaches 8/12=66,666…%. This is not the 62,5% from the textbook example: these are different datasets.</p>\n<p>For the 40 cases, we use the grouped table. The ogive (ojiva) on the right places 0% at the first lower boundary and each cumulative percentage at the upper boundary of its class. For example, at 10,5 it reaches 62,5%. Connecting these points with straight lines does not demonstrate how observations are distributed within each class.</p>\n<figure class=\"tema1-figure\"><a href=\"/study/data-analysis/tema1/en/fig04.svg\" aria-label=\"Open the full-size graph\" class=\"tema1-graph-link\"><img src=\"/study/data-analysis/tema1/en/fig04.svg\" alt=\"Figure SV4. Left: exact accumulation of the mini-case, n=12. Right: grouped ogive, n=40, points (0,5;0), (5,5;32,5), (10,5;62,5), (15,5;82,5), (20,5;92,5), (25,5;97,5), (30,5;100). Both constructions are educational. The ogive uses upper boundaries and is not a copy of Figure 1.5 in the textbook, which places its cumulative polygon above midpoints.\" aria-describedby=\"tema1-caption-4\" width=\"864\" height=\"324\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption id=\"tema1-caption-4\"><strong>Figure SV4.</strong> Left: exact accumulation of the mini-case, n=12. Right: grouped ogive, n=40, points (0,5;0), (5,5;32,5), (10,5;62,5), (15,5;82,5), (20,5;92,5), (25,5;97,5), (30,5;100). Both constructions are educational. The ogive uses upper boundaries and is not a copy of Figure 1.5 in the textbook, which places its cumulative polygon above midpoints.</figcaption><p class=\"ref\"><a href=\"/study/data-analysis/tema1/en/fig04.svg\">Open the full-size graph</a></p></figure>\n<p><strong>Limit of interpretation:</strong> the n=40 table tells us exactly how many integer records are 10 or less. It does not determine how many are 7 or less because 7 lies inside the interval 6–10. Reading an interpolated line as though it were an observed count would be incorrect.</p>\n<p class=\"ref\">Textbook: accumulation, printed p. 26 / PDF p. 24; cumulative graphs, printed pp. 27 and 33–34 / PDF pp. 25 and 31–32; warning about thresholds within a class, printed p. 45 / PDF p. 43. The exact construction and construction at upper boundaries are educational extensions, not original figures.</p>\n<h3>7.5. When classes have unequal widths</h3>\n<p>The histogram represents frequencies through <strong>areas</strong>. If a class is wider, using its frequency directly as its height can visually exaggerate its importance. An appropriate construction for unequal classes is: <strong>height = nᵢ/wᵢ</strong>. The area is then (nᵢ/wᵢ) × wᵢ = nᵢ.</p>\n<p>We regroup the same 40 cases by joining whole classes, without reconstructing individual data:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Apparent class</th><th scope=\"col\">Exact boundaries</th><th scope=\"col\">nᵢ</th><th scope=\"col\">wᵢ</th><th scope=\"col\">Height nᵢ/wᵢ</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>1–10</td><td>0,5–10,5</td><td>25</td><td>10</td><td>2,5</td></tr><tr><td>11–15</td><td>10,5–15,5</td><td>8</td><td>5</td><td>1,6</td></tr><tr><td>16–30</td><td>15,5–30,5</td><td>7</td><td>15</td><td>7/15 ≈ 0,4667</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><figure class=\"tema1-figure\"><a href=\"/study/data-analysis/tema1/en/fig05.svg\" aria-label=\"Open the full-size graph\" class=\"tema1-graph-link\"><img src=\"/study/data-analysis/tema1/en/fig05.svg\" alt=\"Figure SV5. Educational regrouping of Tables 1.5–1.6: areas 25,8,7; sum 40. The Y-axis shows frequency density (densidad de frecuencia), neither absolute frequency nor probability density. The height rule for unequal widths is a methodological extension; the principle of proportional area is explained on printed p. 33 / PDF p. 31.\" aria-describedby=\"tema1-caption-5\" width=\"720\" height=\"338\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption id=\"tema1-caption-5\"><strong>Figure SV5.</strong> Educational regrouping of Tables 1.5–1.6: areas 25,8,7; sum 40. The Y-axis shows frequency density (densidad de frecuencia), neither absolute frequency nor probability density. The height rule for unequal widths is a methodological extension; the principle of proportional area is explained on printed p. 33 / PDF p. 31.</figcaption><p class=\"ref\"><a href=\"/study/data-analysis/tema1/en/fig05.svg\">Open the full-size graph</a></p></figure>\n<details class=\"check\"><summary>Check: does the class with the widest rectangle necessarily have more cases?</summary><p>No. Read its area or its tabulated frequency. Width is the extent of the interval; frequency counts observations.</p></details>\n<p><strong>Retained extension, without further development:</strong> a scatter plot (diagrama de dispersión) can be used for two quantitative variables. An association does not demonstrate causality. Its analysis belongs to later topics; it need not be developed to master the univariate graphs of Tema 1.</p>\n<h2 id=\"distribution\">8. Central tendency, variability and shape</h2>\n<p>A distribution cannot be understood through a single number. We can ask where it is located, how spread out it is and what shape it has. The textbook introduces these properties graphically before calculating their indices.</p>\n<h3>8.1. Centre and variability: different changes</h3>\n<p><strong>Central tendency (tendencia central)</strong> describes the location of central or typical values. <strong>Variability (variabilidad)</strong> describes their spread. A relatively homogeneous (homogénea) distribution has values close to one another; a heterogeneous (heterogénea) distribution has greater spread.</p>\n<p>New educational example: three sets of scores, each with eight observations. A = 4,5,5,6,6,7,7,8; B = 8,9,9,10,10,11,11,12; C = 0,2,4,6,6,8,10,12. They do not represent three actual groups of patients.</p>\n<figure class=\"tema1-figure\"><a href=\"/study/data-analysis/tema1/en/fig06.svg\" aria-label=\"Open the full-size graph\" class=\"tema1-graph-link\"><img src=\"/study/data-analysis/tema1/en/fig06.svg\" alt=\"Figure SV6. Each point is an observation; repeated values are stacked. Vertical position only separates points; it is not another variable. A and B have the same pattern shifted in location; A and C share centre 6, but C is more spread out. Identical horizontal scales. Conceptual basis: printed pp. 35–38 / PDF pp. 33–36.\" aria-describedby=\"tema1-caption-6\" width=\"720\" height=\"504\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption id=\"tema1-caption-6\"><strong>Figure SV6.</strong> Each point is an observation; repeated values are stacked. Vertical position only separates points; it is not another variable. A and B have the same pattern shifted in location; A and C share centre 6, but C is more spread out. Identical horizontal scales. Conceptual basis: printed pp. 35–38 / PDF pp. 33–36.</figcaption><p class=\"ref\"><a href=\"/study/data-analysis/tema1/en/fig06.svg\">Open the full-size graph</a></p></figure>\n<ol><li><strong>Compare A and B:</strong> adding 4 to every value shifts the centre; it does not change the pattern of spread.</li><li><strong>Compare A and C:</strong> both are symmetric around 6; C occupies a wider range and has more observations farther away.</li><li><strong>Interpret:</strong> sharing a centre does not mean that all participants are similar or that the distributions are identical.</li></ol>\n<p>Calculations of indices will be studied later. Here, we practise recognising properties and describing them in words. A difference between group centres does not mean that every person in one group exceeds every person in the other either: there may be overlap.</p>\n<h3>8.2. Skewness: look at the direction of the tail</h3>\n<p>A <strong>symmetric (simétrica)</strong> distribution has sides that mirror one another around its centre. With <strong>positive skewness (asimetría positiva)</strong>, the longer tail extends towards high values, to the right. With <strong>negative skewness (asimetría negativa)</strong>, it extends towards low values, to the left.</p>\n<p>A difficult examination may produce many low scores and few high ones, giving a right tail. An easy examination may produce the reverse pattern. This is a possible interpretation, not a law: we must look at the actual data.</p>\n<figure class=\"tema1-figure\"><a href=\"/study/data-analysis/tema1/en/fig07.svg\" aria-label=\"Open the full-size graph\" class=\"tema1-graph-link\"><img src=\"/study/data-analysis/tema1/en/fig07.svg\" alt=\"Figure SV7. Three fictional datasets, n=25, scores 0–10. Positive frequencies: 8,6,4,3,2,1,1,0,0,0,0; symmetric: 0,0,1,2,5,9,5,2,1,0,0; negative: the positive frequencies in reverse order. New construction based on the explanation on printed pp. 38–39 / PDF pp. 36–37.\" aria-describedby=\"tema1-caption-7\" width=\"936\" height=\"324\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption id=\"tema1-caption-7\"><strong>Figure SV7.</strong> Three fictional datasets, n=25, scores 0–10. Positive frequencies: 8,6,4,3,2,1,1,0,0,0,0; symmetric: 0,0,1,2,5,9,5,2,1,0,0; negative: the positive frequencies in reverse order. New construction based on the explanation on printed pp. 38–39 / PDF pp. 36–37.</figcaption><p class=\"ref\"><a href=\"/study/data-analysis/tema1/en/fig07.svg\">Open the full-size graph</a></p></figure>\n<div class=\"tema1-box\"><strong>Common mistake:</strong> “positive” does not mean “most results are high” or “a favourable result”. The sign indicates the direction of the tail. In the positive example, values are concentrated at the low end.</div>\n<h3>8.3. Kurtosis and excess kurtosis</h3>\n<p>The textbook introduces <strong>kurtosis or peakedness (curtosis o apuntamiento)</strong>: leptokurtic (leptocúrtica), more peaked; platykurtic (platicúrtica), flatter; mesokurtic (mesocúrtica), intermediate. This image helps you recognise the terminology, but it is not a sufficient definition for every possible distribution.</p>\n<p><strong>Methodological clarification:</strong> kurtosis also characterises the behaviour of the tails. In general, it cannot be deduced solely from the height of the peak. <strong>Kurtosis (curtosis)</strong> and <strong>excess kurtosis (exceso de curtosis)</strong> are related but distinct indices: <strong>excess = kurtosis − 3</strong>. For the normal distribution, kurtosis = 3 and excess = 0; mesokurtosis uses that reference. We will not calculate the fourth moment or sample estimators here.</p>\n<figure class=\"tema1-figure\"><a href=\"/study/data-analysis/tema1/en/fig08.svg\" aria-label=\"Open the full-size graph\" class=\"tema1-graph-link\"><img src=\"/study/data-analysis/tema1/en/fig08.svg\" alt=\"Figure SV8. Idealised mathematical curves, not samples or textbook results. All have centre 0, variance 1 and total area 1; the Y-axis is density. They form a controlled educational comparison: kurtosis 6/3/1,8 and excess 3/0/−1,2. Their shapes and tails are compared using a common scale. The model names and calculations are not additional material to memorise.\" aria-describedby=\"tema1-caption-8\" width=\"720\" height=\"360\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption id=\"tema1-caption-8\"><strong>Figure SV8.</strong> Idealised mathematical curves, not samples or textbook results. All have centre 0, variance 1 and total area 1; the Y-axis is density. They form a controlled educational comparison: kurtosis 6/3/1,8 and excess 3/0/−1,2. Their shapes and tails are compared using a common scale. The model names and calculations are not additional material to memorise.</figcaption><p class=\"ref\"><a href=\"/study/data-analysis/tema1/en/fig08.svg\">Open the full-size graph</a></p></figure>\n<p>When reading statistical software, check whether it displays kurtosis or excess. A value of 0 may mean excess 0 rather than kurtosis 0. In this topic, distinguishing the names and references is sufficient.</p>\n<details class=\"check\"><summary>Check: if software reports excess kurtosis 0, is it saying that kurtosis does not exist?</summary><p>No. Under that convention, excess 0 corresponds to kurtosis 3. Nor does it, by itself, prove that the entire distribution is normal.</p></details>\n<p class=\"ref\">Textbook: centre, variability and shape, printed pp. 35–39 / PDF pp. 33–37; peakedness, printed p. 39 / PDF p. 37. Kurtosis/excess, the normal reference and the warning about tails: additional clarification from <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/eda/section3/eda35b.htm\">NIST/SEMATECH, Measures of Skewness and Kurtosis</a>; no textbook page is assigned to them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"exercises\">9. Exercises for understanding and practice</h2>\n<p>Try to answer without opening the solution. Questions 1–4 practise recording measurements, boundaries and accumulation. The remaining questions extend practice of the textbook content with tasks written by Sensus Vitae. The cited pages provide the conceptual basis, not the location of these new formulations.</p>\n<h3>9.1. Four retained tasks</h3>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><h4>1. Reaction time</h4><p>Reaction time is recorded in milliseconds. What is its scale and what type of variable is it?</p><details><summary>Solution and explanation</summary><p><strong>Ratio-scaled and continuous.</strong> Duration has a true zero; it can take intermediate times even if the device rounds to milliseconds. Integer recording does not turn it into a discrete count. Zero on the scale represents absence of duration; a record rounded to zero does not prove an exactly zero physical time.</p><p class=\"ref\">Textbook: printed p. 18 / PDF p. 16; reaction-time solution, printed p. 45 / PDF p. 43.</p></details></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><h4>2. Recording with two decimal places</h4><p>A score rounded to two decimal places is recorded as 18,56. Find its exact boundaries.</p><details><summary>Solution and explanation</summary><p>u=0,01; u/2=0,005. Lower boundary: 18,56−0,005=<strong>18,555</strong>; upper boundary: 18,56+0,005=<strong>18,565</strong>. Under our convention, [18,555;18,565). The common mistake is adding 0,01 rather than half a unit.</p><p class=\"ref\">Textbook: printed p. 45 / PDF p. 43.</p></details></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><h4>3. Width of 10–12</h4><p>The class 10–12 contains integer records. What is its exact width?</p><details><summary>Solution and explanation</summary><p>LIE=9,5; LSE=12,5; w=12,5−9,5=<strong>3</strong>. Subtracting 12−10 gives 2, the difference between apparent limits, not the exact width.</p><p class=\"ref\">Textbook: printed p. 44 / PDF p. 42.</p></details></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><h4>4. Anxiety of 10 or less</h4><p>In the textbook's table of 40 students, what percentage has scores of 10 or less?</p><details><summary>Solution and explanation</summary><p>First two classes: 13+12=25; 25/40=0,625; <strong>62,5%</strong>. Using only the second row would give 30%, which answers “from 6 to 10”, not “10 or less”.</p><p class=\"ref\">Textbook: printed pp. 29–30 / PDF pp. 27–28.</p></details></div>\n<h3>9.2. Concepts and decisions</h3>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><h4>5. Population, sample and statistic</h4><p>We want to describe everyone enrolled in a course in 2026/27. We observe 80 students, and 24 work. Identify the population, the sample and the nature of the resulting 30%.</p><details><summary>Solution and explanation</summary><p>Population: everyone enrolled in the defined course and year. Sample: the 80 observed students. 24/80=30% is a <strong>statistic</strong>. It is neither the population nor a known parameter of everyone enrolled. Key question: “In which set was it calculated?”</p><p class=\"ref\">Textbook: printed pp. 9–10 / PDF pp. 7–8. New example.</p></details></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><h4>6. Nominal scale — three alternatives</h4><p>Codes 1 and 2 indicate two different programmes. A) Interval. B) Nominal. C) Ratio.</p><details><summary>Solution and explanation</summary><p><strong>B.</strong> They identify categories, not quantities. A would require equal quantitative differences; C would additionally require an absolute zero and interpretable ratios. Changing the codes to 7 and 9 would not change the treatment received.</p><p class=\"ref\">Textbook: printed pp. 12–13 / PDF pp. 10–11.</p></details></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><h4>7. An ordered item — three alternatives</h4><p>A response ranges from 1 = strongly disagree to 4 = strongly agree. Without further justification, A) nominal; B) ordinal; C) interval.</p><details><summary>Solution and explanation</summary><p><strong>B.</strong> There is order, but equal distances have not been established. A loses the existing order; C assumes distances that have not been demonstrated. Do not confuse an item with any total test score.</p><p class=\"ref\">Textbook: exercise on printed p. 40 / PDF p. 38; explanation on printed p. 43 / PDF p. 41. New formulation.</p></details></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><h4>8. Recoding</h4><p>We convert “hours of study” into “less than 3 hours” / “3 or more”. What transformation have we made? What information do we lose?</p><details><summary>Solution and explanation</summary><p>A <strong>dichotomisation (dicotomización)</strong> using a cut-off point. We no longer distinguish 3 hours from 8: both fall in the second category. The two categories can be ordered by time, but the codes do not preserve the original differences. The threshold is educational, not a clinical or academic recommendation.</p><p class=\"ref\">Textbook: printed p. 14 / PDF p. 12.</p></details></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><h4>9. Operational definition</h4><p>Explain why “a score interpreted as level of knowledge” and “number of correct answers” should not automatically receive the same classification.</p><details><summary>Solution and explanation</summary><p>Under the textbook convention, level of knowledge measured by a score is treated as <strong>interval-scaled</strong>; zero does not imply complete absence of knowledge. The number of correct answers is a <strong>ratio-scaled, discrete</strong> count: zero means no correct answers to those questions. We classify what is defined and recorded, rather than only its general name.</p><p class=\"ref\">Textbook: printed p. 17 / PDF p. 15; printed p. 42 / PDF p. 40.</p></details></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><h4>10. Matrix and missing values</h4><p>Read case 5 in the mini-case. Then imagine that the record of hours is missing. Can you retain 2,5 as though you had observed it or replace it with 0?</p><details><summary>Solution and explanation</summary><p>Case 5 has group 1, anxiety 7, errors 0 and hours 2,5 in the complete matrix. If that value were genuinely missing in another version, it would need to be marked as missing. Do not retain or substitute a value without a basis. State the number of valid values used in the analysis.</p><p class=\"ref\">Textbook: printed pp. 19–20 / PDF pp. 17–18. New hypothetical scenario.</p></details></div>\n<h3>9.3. From records to an interpretation</h3>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><h4>11. Construct a table</h4><p>Without looking at §5, construct nᵢ, pᵢ and nₐ for the mini-case errors: 0,1,1,2,0,3,1,2,2,4,3,1. What percentage has more than two errors?</p><details><summary>Solution and explanation</summary><p>For 0,1,2,3,4: nᵢ=<strong>2,4,3,2,1</strong>; pᵢ=<strong>2/12,4/12,3/12,2/12,1/12</strong>; nₐ=<strong>2,6,9,11,12</strong>. More than two: 2+1=3 cases, <strong>25%</strong>. This is the complement of 75% with two or fewer. Before interpreting, check that the sum is 12.</p><p class=\"ref\">Textbook: definitions, printed p. 26 / PDF p. 24. New data.</p></details></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><h4>12. Polytomy and accumulation</h4><p>In the low/medium/high recoding, how many categories are there? What percentage is “medium or below”? Can we accumulate in the same way for three diagnoses without an order?</p><details><summary>Solution and explanation</summary><p>Three categories: a polytomous variable, here <strong>ordinal</strong>. “Medium or below”: (4+6)/12=<strong>83,333…%</strong>. Do not accumulate unordered diagnoses with that interpretation: the sum would depend on an arbitrary order.</p><p class=\"ref\">Textbook: printed pp. 13–14 / PDF pp. 11–12; printed p. 26 / PDF p. 24.</p></details></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><h4>13. Recover a frequency from a cumulative frequency</h4><p>A cumulative graph of 150 cases shows 15,36,87,123,150. What is the relative frequency of the third class? A) 0,58. B) 0,34. C) 0,82.</p><details><summary>Solution and explanation</summary><p><strong>B.</strong> First, recover its ordinary frequency: 87−36=51. Then: 51/150=<strong>0,34</strong>. A is 87/150, cumulative up to the third class; C is 123/150, cumulative up to the fourth. The complete ordinary frequencies are 15,21,51,36,27 and sum to 150.</p><p class=\"ref\">Values from the textbook graph: printed p. 41 / PDF p. 39; solution: printed p. 44 / PDF p. 42. The question is reformulated.</p></details></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><h4>14. PM and limits</h4><p>A class of integer records has PM=11 and width 3. Obtain exact boundaries and apparent limits.</p><details><summary>Solution and explanation</summary><p>Half the width = 1,5. Exact boundaries: 11−1,5=<strong>9,5</strong> and 11+1,5=<strong>12,5</strong>. For integers: apparent limits <strong>10–12</strong>. Verification: PM=11 and 12,5−9,5=3.</p><p class=\"ref\">Textbook: printed p. 44 / PDF p. 42.</p></details></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><h4>15. A question the table cannot answer</h4><p>Does the n=40 anxiety table allow us to know exactly how many students scored 7 or less?</p><details><summary>Solution and explanation</summary><p><strong>No.</strong> We know the 13 in 1–5, but the 12 in 6–10 are not separated by scores. They could be distributed in different ways. Do not read an interpolation of the ogive as an actual count. For 10 or less, we do know there are 25 cases.</p><p class=\"ref\">Textbook: grouping, printed pp. 28–30 / PDF pp. 26–28; limitation of an internal threshold, printed p. 45 / PDF p. 43.</p></details></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><h4>16. The area of a histogram</h4><p>In Figure SV5, one class has nᵢ=8 and width 5. Another has nᵢ=7 and width 15. Which heights make their areas 8 and 7?</p><details><summary>Solution and explanation</summary><p><strong>8/5=1,6</strong> and <strong>7/15≈0,4667</strong>. Check: 1,6×5=8; (7/15)×15=7. The second rectangle is wider but represents fewer cases. Do not confuse frequency density with a proportion.</p><p class=\"ref\">Principle of proportional area: printed p. 33 / PDF p. 31. Rule applied to unequal classes: additional methodological clarification.</p></details></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><h4>17. Two changes in distribution</h4><p>In Figure SV6, compare A with B and A with C. What changes in each comparison?</p><details><summary>Solution and explanation</summary><p>A→B: <strong>centre</strong>, with the same pattern shifted. A→C: <strong>spread</strong>, retaining centre 6. Sharing a centre does not make the distributions identical. Always compare using the same graphical scale.</p><p class=\"ref\">Textbook: printed pp. 35–38 / PDF pp. 33–36. New data.</p></details></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><h4>18. Sign and convention</h4><p>a) There are many low values and a tail towards high values: which skewness does this illustrate? b) If excess is 0, what is kurtosis under the stated relationship?</p><details><summary>Solution and explanation</summary><p>a) <strong>Positive</strong>: the tail extends to the right. b) <strong>3</strong>, because excess=kurtosis−3. Excess 0 does not, by itself, prove normality or mean absence of kurtosis.</p><p class=\"ref\">Skewness: printed pp. 38–39 / PDF pp. 36–37. Kurtosis/excess: NIST, extension with no textbook page assigned.</p></details></div>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">10. Synthesis and preparation for Tema 1</h2>\n<p>The core of the topic is a chain of decisions: <strong>question → variable and measurement → matrix → frequency distribution → graph → interpretation</strong>. If the variable is incorrectly defined or coded, a correct calculation can answer the wrong question.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">After studying, I should be able to…</th><th scope=\"col\">How to check</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Define population and sample</td><td>Name exactly whom the question concerns and whom we observed.</td></tr><tr><td>Distinguish parameter and statistic</td><td>Ask in which set the number was obtained.</td></tr><tr><td>Classify variables and scales</td><td>Explain which comparisons each record permits.</td></tr><tr><td>Read and review a matrix</td><td>Identify rows, columns, codes and missing values.</td></tr><tr><td>Construct frequencies</td><td>Count, divide by the correct total and verify sums.</td></tr><tr><td>Interpret cumulative frequencies</td><td>Distinguish “exactly” from “up to”; recover by differences.</td></tr><tr><td>Work with classes</td><td>Calculate limits, PM and width; recognise open-ended classes.</td></tr><tr><td>Read graphs</td><td>Identify variables, axes and frequency type before concluding.</td></tr><tr><td>Recognise centre, spread and shape</td><td>Compare graphs without confusing properties or conventions.</td></tr><tr><td>Recognise information limits</td><td>Do not invent individual values or causality from a summary.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p><strong>For UNED-style practice:</strong> combine conceptual questions and small problems with three alternatives. Do not memorise only the answer: explain the error represented by each incorrect alternative. Use the textbook to review concepts you still cannot justify.</p>\n<p>The public 2026/27 course guide identifies this Tema 1 and assesses theoretical and practical questions. This text is supplementary study material, not a guarantee of passing or a substitute for the teaching team's instructions. Restricted Ágora instructions were not reviewed during this preparation.</p>\n<p>Systematic calculations of indices of central tendency and position are developed in Tema 2, and those of variability and shape in Tema 3. We do not introduce them prematurely. The present chapter prepares the necessary language and organisation.</p>\n<p class=\"ref\">Textbook: Summary, printed p. 40 / PDF p. 38; topic organisation and assessment: public UNED 2026/27 course guide.</p>\n<h2 id=\"terms\">11. Glossary and sources</h2>\n<h3>11.1. UNED terminology with definitions</h3>\n<p>The Spanish terms help you recognise the vocabulary used by UNED. English equivalents help you recognise sources and software; meanings are explained in English. The label “additional” prevents us from attributing clarifications to the textbook when they have not been located there as such.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table class=\"glossary\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">UNED term</th><th scope=\"col\">English</th><th scope=\"col\">Brief definition</th><th scope=\"col\">Printed page / PDF</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Población</td><td>Population</td><td>Set of units defined by the study question.</td><td>10 / 8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Muestra</td><td>Sample</td><td>Subset of the population that is observed.</td><td>10 / 8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Parámetro</td><td>Parameter</td><td>Numerical characteristic of a population.</td><td>10 / 8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Estadístico</td><td>Sample statistic</td><td>Numerical characteristic calculated in a sample.</td><td>10 / 8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Estadística descriptiva</td><td>Descriptive statistics</td><td>Organisation and summary of observed data.</td><td>7–10 / 5–8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Estadística inferencial</td><td>Inferential statistics</td><td>Probabilistic procedures for drawing inferences about a population from a sample.</td><td>7–10 / 5–8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Variable; Xᵢ</td><td>Variable; observed value</td><td>Recorded characteristic; Xᵢ is its value in case i.</td><td>10–11 / 8–9</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Medición</td><td>Measurement</td><td>Assignment of numbers to characteristics or objects according to rules.</td><td>11–12 / 9–10</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Definición operativa</td><td>Operational definition</td><td>Specification of how the variable is defined and recorded.</td><td>17 / 15</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Escala nominal</td><td>Nominal scale</td><td>Classifies; permits equality/difference without a quantitative order.</td><td>12–13 / 10–11</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Escala ordinal</td><td>Ordinal scale</td><td>Classifies and orders; does not guarantee equal distances.</td><td>14–15 / 12–13</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Escala de intervalo</td><td>Interval scale</td><td>Permits equal differences; zero is conventional.</td><td>15–16 / 13–14</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Escala de razón</td><td>Ratio scale</td><td>Permits differences and ratios with an absolute zero.</td><td>16–17 / 14–15</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cualitativa / categórica</td><td>Categorical variable</td><td>In the Tema 1 classification, a nominal variable consisting of categories.</td><td>13,18 / 11,16</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cuasicuantitativa</td><td>Ordinal variable</td><td>The textbook's name for an ordinal variable.</td><td>14,18 / 12,16</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cuantitativa</td><td>Quantitative variable</td><td>Variable measured on an interval or ratio scale in the textbook's classification.</td><td>17–18 / 15–16</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dicotómica</td><td>Dichotomous variable</td><td>Has exactly two possible categories or values.</td><td>13 / 11</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Politómica</td><td>Polytomous variable</td><td>Has more than two possible categories or values.</td><td>13 / 11</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dicotomizar / politomizar</td><td>Dichotomise / polytomise</td><td>Transform values into two or more than two categories according to rules.</td><td>14 / 12</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Discreta</td><td>Discrete variable</td><td>Has separated values; does not permit intermediate values between consecutive possible values.</td><td>18 / 16</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Continua</td><td>Continuous variable</td><td>Can take intermediate values within its range.</td><td>18 / 16</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Matriz de datos</td><td>Data matrix</td><td>Organisation with cases in rows and variables in columns.</td><td>19–21 / 17–19</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Codificación</td><td>Coding</td><td>Rules for recording categories, values and missing data.</td><td>19–20 / 17–18</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dato perdido</td><td>Missing value</td><td>Value not recorded or observed; it is not equivalent to zero.</td><td>20 / 18</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dato atípico</td><td>Outlier</td><td>Value very different from the others that requires review.</td><td>20 / 18</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Frecuencia absoluta; nᵢ</td><td>Absolute frequency</td><td>Number of cases for a value or class.</td><td>26 / 24</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Frecuencia relativa; pᵢ</td><td>Relative frequency</td><td>Proportion of cases: nᵢ/n.</td><td>26 / 24</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Porcentaje; Pᵢ</td><td>Percentage</td><td>Relative frequency multiplied by 100.</td><td>26 / 24</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Frecuencia acumulada</td><td>Cumulative frequency</td><td>Sum up to a value or class in a meaningful order.</td><td>26 / 24</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Límites aparentes; LIA, LSA</td><td>Apparent class limits</td><td>Endpoints expressed in the recorded units.</td><td>29–32 / 27–30</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Límites exactos; LIE, LSE</td><td>Class boundaries</td><td>Continuous boundaries taking the recording unit into account.</td><td>29–32 / 27–30</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Punto medio; PM</td><td>Class midpoint</td><td>Half the sum of the two limits of a class.</td><td>32,46 / 30,44</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Amplitud de intervalo</td><td>Class width</td><td>Difference between upper and lower exact boundaries.</td><td>32,45 / 30,43</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Intervalo abierto</td><td>Open-ended class</td><td>Extreme class with one unspecified boundary.</td><td>32 / 30</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Diagrama de barras / sectores</td><td>Bar chart / pie chart</td><td>Representations of frequencies through heights or angles.</td><td>23–24 / 21–22</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Histograma</td><td>Histogram</td><td>Contiguous rectangles with areas proportional to frequencies.</td><td>33 / 31</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Diagrama de líneas / polígono de frecuencias</td><td>Frequency polygon</td><td>Points above values or PM, connected to show frequencies.</td><td>34 / 32</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tendencia central</td><td>Central tendency</td><td>Central or typical location of a distribution.</td><td>36–37 / 34–35</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Variabilidad; homogénea / heterogénea</td><td>Variability</td><td>Spread; relatively less / greater spread of the values.</td><td>37–38 / 35–36</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Simetría / asimetría</td><td>Symmetry / skewness</td><td>Mirror balance or imbalance; the skewness sign follows the direction of the tail.</td><td>38–39 / 36–37</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Curtosis / apuntamiento</td><td>Kurtosis</td><td>Introduced through shape in the textbook; additional precision about tails.</td><td>39 / 37; additional precision from NIST</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Exceso de curtosis</td><td>Excess kurtosis</td><td>Kurtosis minus 3; normal reference 0.</td><td>Additional: NIST, no textbook page</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Leptocúrtica / mesocúrtica / platicúrtica</td><td>Leptokurtic / mesokurtic / platykurtic</td><td>Shape terminology; with the additional precision through indices: kurtosis greater than / equal to / less than 3.</td><td>39 / 37; additional reference through indices</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Variable independiente / dependiente</td><td>Independent / dependent variable</td><td>Condition manipulated or assigned / response recorded in an experiment.</td><td>Methodological extension; context of Example 1.1, 6–7 / 4–5</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Densidad de frecuencia</td><td>Frequency density</td><td>nᵢ/width; height that makes area equal to nᵢ.</td><td>Extension; area principle: 33 / 31</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ojiva</td><td>Ogive</td><td>In SV4, cumulative curve at upper boundaries; it does not reconstruct individual values.</td><td>Additional educational construction; accumulation: 26 / 24</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><h3 id=\"sources\">11.2. Sources and limitations of the text</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Main source:</strong> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1R_MKDRXZTQjR75WWt9oHrkHcslPV7YyD/view?usp=drivesdk\">AD H1.pdf — Conceptos básicos y organización de datos</a>. A 44-page PDF excerpt, printed pp. 3–46. Pages have been verified in the excerpt; its publisher's title page is not included.</li>\n<li><strong>Official bibliography:</strong> Suárez Falcón, J. C., Recio Saboya, P., San Luis Costas, M. C., and Pozo Cabanillas, P. (2019). <em>Introducción al análisis de datos: Aplicaciones en psicología y ciencias de la salud</em> (2nd ed.). Sanz y Torres. ISBN 978-84-17765-42-2. The 2026/27 guide lists it as a basic textbook; it also accepts the first edition from 2017. The scan is not identified as a specific edition without its title page.</li>\n<li><strong>Syllabus and assessment:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.uned.es/universidad/inicio/estudios/grados/grado-en-psicologia/asignaturas.html?codAsignatura=62011037&amp;codTitulacion=6201&amp;idContenido=1\">Public UNED 2026/27 course guide, course 62011037</a>, consulted during the academic audit. Restricted Ágora instructions have not been reviewed.</li>\n<li><strong>Additional terminological precision:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/eda/section3/eda35b.htm\">NIST/SEMATECH e-Handbook, Measures of Skewness and Kurtosis</a>: kurtosis, excess and tails.</li>\n<li><strong>Retained framework of scales:</strong> Stevens (1946), as a historical reference from the published version. The teaching and page references assigned here rely on the treatment in the Tema 1 textbook. Warnings about instruments, causality, selection and rounding are labelled as methodological clarifications.</li></ol>\n<p>The n=40 table and n=150 cumulative counts come from the textbook. The n=12 mini-case, the A/B/C datasets, the three n=25 patterns and all eight figures are new educational material. SV8 uses mathematical densities of a standard normal distribution, a centred Laplace distribution with scale 1/√2 and a uniform distribution between −√3 and √3: all have variance 1. This note allows the graph to be verified; the models are not part of the memorisation objectives for Tema 1.</p>\n"
